# The king's door, at the end of the hall: sealed stone, and the
# sealers' riddle cut into its face. Fires when somebody walks up
# (MapComponent_TSC_Barrow.TryDoorRiddle), and GRANTS the regalia quest:
# the mound names its own price at its own door.

dialogue TSC_Dialogue_KingsDoor

== start ==
The hall ends at a door of dressed stone with no bar, no ring, and no seam wide enough for a knife. It was not made to be forced. It was made to be answered.

Set into its face at chest height are three shallow indentations: a small circle. A flat disc, palm-sized. A crescent, lying horns-up.

Above them runs a riddle, cut deep - and cut in a hand eight centuries younger than the panels along the hall. The maker carved the roads. The sealers carved the door, on their way out, for whoever came after.

* [Read it.] -> riddle

== riddle ==
_BRING THE THING THAT KEEPS HIS NAME._

_BRING THE THING THAT KEEPS HIS HOURS._

_BRING THE THING THAT HOLDS HIS SHAPE._

_A WHO, A WHEN, AND A WHAT. KNOCK WITH ALL THREE, OR NOT AT ALL._

* "A who, a when, and a what." -> answer

== answer ==
Three parts of a person, and three shapes cut to hold them.

The circle for the name: a ring, the kind a man swears on. The disc for the hours: something that counts time, and has been counting a long while. And the crescent for the shape, which is the odd one out, because a crescent is not a body. It is a mold.

Three regalia, carried out of this mound behind a coffin nine hundred years ago. The indentations are shallow - nothing is meant to be *left* here. Press each keeping into its shape and the door will open.

The door is younger than every grave behind it. For a hundred years this passage stood open and kings were buried here; the last men out were the ones who closed it.

The shards in the company's packs are already leaning three different directions, like hounds shown a scent. They know where each one went.

* [Follow the lean.] -> end
    do flag(TSC_RegaliaNeeded); give_quest(TSC_Act5_Regalia); grant_xp(60)
